# 1.24 Key Takeaways

The mathematical foundation of Bayesian optimization can be summarized as follows.

1. We have an unknown objective:

$$
f(\mathbf{x}).
$$

2. Evaluating \(f\) may be expensive.

3. We therefore cannot evaluate every possible \(\mathbf{x}\).

4. Experimental observations may contain noise:

$$
y=f(\mathbf{x})+\epsilon.
$$

5. Probability allows us to represent uncertainty.

6. Mean describes the center of a distribution.

7. Variance describes uncertainty or spread.

8. Covariance describes how two quantities vary together.

9. A Gaussian process extends these ideas from random variables to unknown functions.

10. Bayesian updating allows our beliefs about the unknown function to change after observing data.

11. Bayesian optimization repeatedly uses the current model to decide where the next experiment should be performed.

The fundamental workflow is:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Unknown function}
\rightarrow
\text{Probabilistic model}
\rightarrow
\text{Prediction + uncertainty}
\rightarrow
\text{Decision}
\rightarrow
\text{New observation}
\rightarrow
\text{Updated model}
}
$$

This framework will serve as the mathematical foundation for the following sections.
